infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title list vs set: membership is the performance decision
  desc Same 50,000 entries — x in c is O(n) on a list but O(1) on a set
  compares
    - label list — x in lst is an O(n) scan
      children
        - label 50,000 entries -> up to 50,000 comparisons
          desc in walks the elements one by one; the JS-array reflex, and the wrong tool for membership
        - label Millions of checks -> O(n^2) overall
          desc A membership test in a hot loop degrades to accidentally quadratic as the list grows
        - label No automatic dedup
          desc A list keeps duplicates; finding them is another O(n) scan
    - label set — x in s is an O(1) hash lookup
      children
        - label 50,000 entries -> about 1 hash probe
          desc Members live in hash buckets, so lookup time stays flat however many entries
        - label Stays O(1) as data grows
          desc One character of intent — make seen a set — and the p99 collapses
        - label Unique members for free
          desc Adding a duplicate is a no-op; dedup is built in, with no scan needed
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
